{0 OCaml Telegram Bot API Library (Eio)}

An ergonomic, type-safe Telegram Bot API client library for OCaml 5, built on Eio for structured concurrency.

{1 Overview}

[ocaml_telegram_eio] provides a complete, production-ready toolkit for building Telegram bots in OCaml:

- {b Fully type-safe API}: All Telegram Bot API types and methods with precise OCaml types
- {b Effects-based concurrency}: Built on Eio for elegant async/await patterns without monads
- {b Zero-cost abstractions}: Compile-time guarantees with minimal runtime overhead
- {b Comprehensive}: Long polling, webhooks, file uploads/downloads, payments, and more
- {b Well-tested}: Extensive test suite including unit, property, integration, and concurrency tests

{1 Getting Started}

New to the library? Start here:

{{!page-getting_started}Getting Started Guide} - Complete installation and setup tutorial with your first bot

{2 Quick Example}

{[
open Eio.Std
open Ocaml_telegram_eio

let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->

  (* Create a bot client *)
  let token = Sys.getenv "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN" in
  let client = Client.create ~env ~token in

  (* Define message handler *)
  let handle_update update =
    match update.Update.message with
    | Some msg ->
        let chat_id = msg.Message.chat.Chat.id in
        let text = Option.value msg.Message.text ~default:"" in

        (* Echo the message back *)
        let req = Api.send_message ~chat_id ~text () in
        let* _response = Api.call client req in
        Ok ()
    | None -> Ok ()
  in

  (* Start long polling *)
  Polling.run client handle_update
]}

{1 Core Modules}

{2 Client & API}

- {!Telegram.Client}: HTTP client for making API calls
- {!Telegram.Api}: Type-safe API method builders
- {!Telegram_generated.Gen_types}: Generated Telegram types
- {!Telegram.Request}: Request representation
- {!Telegram.Response}: Response handling

{2 Update Intake}

- {!Tg.Polling}: Long polling runner with offset management
- {!Tg.Webhook}: Webhook server with signature verification

{2 High-Level Bot DSL}

- {!Tg.Bot}: Bot context and routing
- {!Tg.Session}: Type-safe session storage
- {!Tg.Keyboard}: Reply keyboard and inline keyboard builders

{2 Files & Media}

- {!Tg.Download}: File download utilities
- {!Tg.Upload}: Streaming multipart file uploads
- {!Telegram.Input_file}: File input abstraction
- {!Telegram.Media_group}: Media group handling
- {!Tg.Album}: Album composition helpers
- {!Tg.Large_file}: Large file chunking strategies

{2 Payments & Currency}

- {!Telegram.Money}: Money type with minor units
- {!Telegram.Currency}: Currency definitions and formatting

{2 Utilities}

- {!Telegram.Error}: Error types and retryability
- {!Telegram.Retry}: Exponential backoff retry logic
- {!Telegram.Limits}: Telegram API limits and validation
- {!Telegram.Id}: Type-safe ID wrappers
- {!Telegram.Parse_mode}: HTML/Markdown formatting modes
- {!Telegram.Chat_action}: Chat action indicators
- {!Telegram.Units}: Type-safe units (seconds, bytes)

{1 Architecture}

{2 Type Safety}

All Telegram types are represented with precise OCaml types. Optional fields use [option], sum types use polymorphic variants, and IDs are wrapped in phantom types to prevent mixing different ID kinds.

{2 Error Handling}

The library uses [Result.t] for error handling, distinguishing between retryable errors (network issues, rate limits) and non-retryable errors (invalid parameters):

{[
type ('a, 'e) result = ('a, 'e) Result.t

let* response = Api.call client request in
match response with
| Ok data -> (* handle success *)
| Error err when Error.is_retryable err -> (* retry *)
| Error err -> (* permanent failure *)
]}

{2 Concurrency with Eio}

Built on Eio's structured concurrency model:

{[
Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->
  (* Spawn concurrent fibers *)
  Eio.Fiber.fork ~sw (fun () -> handle_updates ());
  Eio.Fiber.fork ~sw (fun () -> send_notifications ());

  (* Switch waits for all fibers to complete *)
  (* Cleanup handlers run automatically *)
]}

